# LiwanOS AI is optional and off by default. To enable it add these to deploy/.env and start with
# COMPOSE_PROFILES=ai. Without COMPOSE_PROFILES=ai the ai service does not exist and /ai stays hidden.
COMPOSE_PROFILES=ai
NUXT_AI_SERVICE_URL=http://ai:8600
# LiwanOS AI provider for this deployment: anthropic, openai, ollama, openrouter or openai_compatible (any OpenAI-compatible
# endpoint, which needs AI_BASE_URL). AI_API_KEY is required except for ollama; with openrouter AI_MODEL is the OpenRouter
# model id. Sovereign customers use ollama with a local AI_BASE_URL.
# These four are read by deploy/provision.sh only: it writes them to data/ai/platform-provider.json (mode 600), the one source
# the ai container reads. No container receives them. To change the provider, edit them and run `python3 ai/platform_provider.py .env data/ai/platform-provider.json`
# from deploy/; the ai service picks the new file up within seconds (no restart).
# Optional, openrouter only (attribution headers HTTP-Referer and X-Title): AI_OPENROUTER_REFERER, AI_OPENROUTER_TITLE.
AI_PROVIDER=ollama
AI_MODEL=qwen2.5:7b
AI_BASE_URL=http://ollama:11434
AI_API_KEY=
AI_OPENROUTER_REFERER=
# Optional, true or false: ask the provider's stream for token counts. Needed (true) for a custom AI_BASE_URL whose server accepts
# stream_options.include_usage, otherwise its calls carry no token counts and the token limits cannot be enforced. Leave empty for the default.
AI_STREAM_USAGE=
AI_OPENROUTER_TITLE=
AI_RUN_RETENTION_DAYS=30
# Knowledge retrieval embeddings: openai (any OpenAI-compatible endpoint) or ollama. Empty leaves knowledge off. The
# embedding host must also be in AI_EGRESS_HOSTS; provision.sh adds it when it fills that line.
AI_EMBEDDING_PROVIDER=
AI_EMBEDDING_MODEL=
AI_EMBEDDING_BASE_URL=
AI_EMBEDDING_API_KEY=
# Hosts the ai container may reach: it has no internet route of its own, and ai-egress allows only this list.
# Comma separated. A bare host allows HTTPS (CONNECT, port 443); host:port allows exactly that port, which is how a
# local model server (ollama:11434) is reached. Empty denies everything. provision.sh fills it from AI_PROVIDER and
# AI_BASE_URL when it is empty: openrouter.ai, api.anthropic.com, api.openai.com, or the AI_BASE_URL host and port.
AI_EGRESS_HOSTS=
# AI_DB_PASSWORD, AI_SERVICE_TOKEN and the key-encryption pair are generated by `deploy/ai/ensure_secrets.sh` when `deploy/provision.sh`
# runs with COMPOSE_PROFILES=ai (64 hex characters, deploy/.env mode 600). Their values are never printed.
# Do not rotate them casually: the token is shared by `web` and `ai`, so rotating it needs both restarted
# together; the password is used by the `liwan_ai` role on a fresh db volume (and by AI_DATABASE_URL).
# Used by the liwan_ai database role; it must stay URL-safe because it is placed inside AI_DATABASE_URL.
AI_DB_PASSWORD=
# REQUIRED: the service refuses to start without it (docker-compose.yml passes it through).
# Its own database URL for the liwan_ai role: user liwan_ai, host db, port 5432, database liwan_ai, using the secret above.
# (kept out of docker-compose.yml so no password name sits next to a literal in a tracked file).
AI_DATABASE_URL=
# The service database above holds release control state only. Each tenant's AI state lives in schema
# liwan_ai inside that tenant's own database: docker-compose.yml sets LIWAN_TENANCY_MODE=dedicated and
# AI_TENANT_DATABASE=${DB_NAME}; docker-compose.shared.yml switches to the tenant registry.
# REQUIRED, shared secret between web and ai, min 24 characters (generated by ensure_secrets.sh).
# AI_DEV_INSECURE (local development only) removes this requirement; it must stay unset in deployments.
AI_SERVICE_TOKEN=
# REQUIRED for web, generated by ensure_secrets.sh: the key of the AI tokens that carry mobile numbers and emails past
# the provider (spec 2026-11-04 §6.12). Only `web` receives it, never `ai`. Rotating it makes the tokens already stored
# in conversations show as tokens instead of their values.
AI_TOKEN_KEY=
# Encrypt each tenant's own provider key in its liwan_ai schema (pgcrypto, AES-256). `id:secret` pairs, comma
# separated, and the id new keys use. ensure_secrets.sh generates `k1:<64 hex>` and `k1`. To rotate, add a new pair,
# point the id at it, restart, then run `python -m liwan_ai_service.tenant_config --rotate`; keep the old pair until
# every backup that may be restored was taken after the rotation (deploy/OPERATIONS.md).
AI_KEY_ENCRYPTION_KEYS=
AI_KEY_ENCRYPTION_KEY_ID=
